+# The shader cache file (D115)
+
+One indexed file holds everything NativeXRenderer compiles for a game, and the renderer prepares all of it
+before the game draws its first frame, while a status screen shows the progress. On the first launch a game can
+also hand over every shader it ships, so they are compiled before the player ever sees them. This document is for
+whoever adds it to another ReXGlue game (section 1, about an hour) or changes it (sections 2–4). Mad Riders is the
+first game with it (`mad-riders-recomp`: `src/mad_riders_app.h`, `src/shader_scan.cpp`).
+
+Where the code is: `src/storage.{h,cpp}` (the file), `src/shaders.{h,cpp}` (translations from stored SPIR-V, the
+worker boost), `src/draw.cpp` (`OpenStorage`, `PrepareStep`, `QueueGameTranslations`, `SavePipelineCache`),
+`src/renderer.cpp` (`PrepareShaderCache`: the hold), `include/native_x_renderer/native_x_renderer.h`
+(`SetShaderCache`, `GetShaderCacheStatus`), `include/native_x_renderer/shader_sources.h` (finding a game's shaders),
+`include/native_x_renderer/shader_cache_screen.h` (the status screen).
+
+> Until it is upstream in ElGuslml/NativeXRenderer, this is `patches/native_x_renderer/0002-*.patch` in
+> mad-riders-recomp: apply it after 0001 (both touch `renderer.cpp`) to the submodule, as that project's CI does.
+
+## 1. Adding it to a game
+
+Without any game code the cache already works: it is `<cache root>/native_x_renderer/<title id>.cache`, what the
+game uses is kept, and the next start prepares it before the first frame (with no screen, the window stays black
+meanwhile). Three steps make it complete:
+
+1. **Put the file where the player expects it, and hand over the game's shaders** — before
+   `native_x_renderer::Initialize`, usually in `OnPostSetup`:
+
+   ```cpp
+   #include <native_x_renderer/native_x_renderer.h>
+   #include <native_x_renderer/shader_sources.h>
+
+   namespace sources = native_x_renderer::shader_sources;
+   native_x_renderer::ShaderCacheSource source;
+   source.file = rex::filesystem::GetExecutableFolder() / "shaders.cache";
+   const auto data = game_data_root / "media";                     // where the game keeps its shaders
+   const auto files = sources::ListFiles(data, {".xpr", ".fxo"}, /*recursive=*/true);
+   source.game_fingerprint = sources::FingerprintFiles(data, files, /*version=*/1);
+   source.scan = [files](const std::function<void(uint32_t, uint32_t)>& progress) {
+     std::vector<native_x_renderer::GameShader> shaders;
+     for (size_t i = 0; i < files.size(); ++i) {
+       progress(uint32_t(i), uint32_t(files.size()));
+       std::vector<uint8_t> bytes = ReadFile(files[i]);             // inflate first if the game compresses
+       sources::FindXdkShaders(bytes.data(), bytes.size(), shaders);
+     }
+     sources::Deduplicate(shaders);
+     return shaders;
+   };
+   native_x_renderer::SetShaderCache(std::move(source));
+   native_x_renderer::Initialize(profile, graphics, window, runtime()->cache_root());
+   ```
+
+   `game_fingerprint` must be cheap (it is computed at every start) and change when the scan would find
+   something else: names and sizes of the files, plus a version you bump when you improve the scan. `0` (or no
+   `scan`) skips the first-launch build. `scan` runs on a thread of its own, once per fingerprint.
+
+2. **Show the status screen** — in `OnCreateDialogs`:
+
+   ```cpp
+   #include <native_x_renderer/shader_cache_screen.h>
+   native_x_renderer::ShaderCacheScreen::Style style;
+   style.title = "MY GAME";                     // colors: style.accent, bar_left, bar_right, edge (ImU32)
+   screen_ = std::make_unique<native_x_renderer::ShaderCacheScreen>(imgui_drawer, style);
+   ```
+
+   Or write your own: poll `native_x_renderer::GetShaderCacheStatus()` (any thread) in an `ImGuiDialog` and draw
+   while `phase` is between `kOpening` and `kSaving`. `ShaderCachePhaseName(phase)` gives the step's text.
+
+3. **Turn the SDK's own shader storage off** (`store_shaders = false`: it is the Xenos path's, and NativeXRenderer
+   already turns it off at Initialize) and remove old caches if the game had any.
+
+Check in the log: `shader cache <path>: N shaders, N translations and N pipelines from earlier runs`, then
+`the game ships N shaders`, then `shader cache ready in X s: …`. On the next start the same line shows the
+translations made `from the file` and none translated.
+
+## 2. Finding where a game keeps its shaders
+
+The Xbox 360 XDK's shader compiler writes a **shader object**; the game hands it to
+`D3DDevice_CreateVertexShader`/`CreatePixelShader`. Its header is big-endian:
+
+| Offset | Field |
+|---|---|
+| +0 | magic: `0x102A1101` vertex, `0x102A1100` pixel |
+| +4 | virtual size (header, constant table, shader description) |
+| +8 | physical size (the microcode, which the GPU reads) |
+| +24 | offset of the shader description in the virtual part; its first two words are the microcode's offset and size in the physical part |
+
+The profile string (`vs_3_0`, `ps_3_0`) and constant names (`CONST_100`, `s_clr`) sit in the virtual part, which
+makes the objects easy to spot in a hex dump. Engines store them in one of two ways:
+
+- **Whole** (virtual part then physical part): `FindXdkShaders` over the bytes. Typical for loose shader files
+  and uncompressed effect containers.
+- **Split** (the physical parts cut out and kept elsewhere, in the same order): `FindXdkShadersSplit` with the two
+  buffers and the alignment between physical parts. Mad Riders (Chrome Engine 5) does this in its `.rpack`
+  packs: zlib-compressed sections of type 0x30 (objects) and 0x31 (microcode, 32-byte aligned).
+
+Archives usually compress (zlib: `78 9C`/`78 DA` at a section's start; the SDK ships `stb_image.h`, whose
+`stbi_zlib_decode_buffer` inflates with `STB_IMAGE_STATIC` defined) and sometimes encrypt (Chrome Engine's `.pak`
+zips are: their shaders are HLSL sources compiled offline into the `.rpack`s, so the `.pak`s can be skipped).
+
+**Checking what you found against a Xenia dump.** Run the game in Xenia with `dump_shaders = "<folder>"` and play
+a little; then compare. The names are `shader_<hash>.ucode.bin.vert|frag` (and `<hash>_<modification>.*.bin.*`
+for translations), where **hash = XXH3-64 of the microcode as big-endian dwords** (as in guest memory, and as
+NativeXRenderer hashes it); the `.ucode.bin` file itself holds the dwords little-endian, so byte-swap each dword
+before searching for it in game data. Mad Riders: all 204 pixel shaders of a session were among the 495 found.
+
+**Vertex shaders do not match, and that is expected.** The XDK's D3D patches each vertex shader's vertex fetch
+instructions (fetch constant, stride, offset, format) for the vertex declaration it is drawn with, so the
+microcode the GPU gets differs from the shipped one in exactly those words (one variant per layout). The shipped
+vertex shaders are still analyzed (their interpolator outputs drive the pixel shaders' guesses, below), but
+vertex translations and pipelines come from play and are kept in the file.
+
+## 3. What happens at a start
+
+`PrepareShaderCache` (renderer.cpp) runs on the renderer's thread before the first command it executes. The game
+is held meanwhile: its render thread waits in the command processor's signal gate (or in the call itself without
+the renderer's thread). Each loop presents a black frame with the ImGui overlay (the game's dialogs, so its status
+screen) at up to 60 FPS and calls `DrawPath::PrepareStep`, which does about 12 ms of work:
+
+| Phase | Work |
+|---|---|
+| `kOpening` | `PipelineStorage::Open`: the index and the journal read; the driver's pipeline cache handed to DiligentCore. |
+| `kScanning` | First launch only (the file has no `G` record for the game's fingerprint): the game's `scan` on a thread. |
+| `kAnalyzing` | Every stored shader, then every shipped one, analyzed (`ShaderCache::Get`, the SDK's `AnalyzeUcode`). Then queued: every stored translation, the stored pipelines' translations, and the guessed pixel translations of the shipped shaders. |
+| `kTranslating` | The workers make the host shaders: from stored SPIR-V (no translator) or translated (stored as they finish). `SetBoost(true)` lets every core but one work. |
+| `kPipelines` | Every stored pipeline created at once (DiligentCore compiles them asynchronously, through the driver's cache) and waited for. |
+| `kSaving` | If anything new was made: the driver's cache read back and the file written whole (on the save thread). |
+| `kReady` | The game runs. New shaders, translations and pipelines are journaled at each frame end; the file is written whole again with the driver's cache 10 s after new pipelines, at most every 5 minutes (D45, D52). |
+
+**The guessed pixel translations.** A pixel shader's translation depends on its draw: the interpolators the
+vertex shader writes that the pixel shader reads (`interpolator_mask`), centroid sampling (MSAA), PsParamGen, and
+the depth mode (early-Z hint unless alpha test or alpha to coverage). `QueueGameTranslations` makes, for each
+shipped pixel shader, one translation per distinct `writes_interpolators()` of the shipped vertex shaders
+(intersected with what it reads) and per depth mode it can need, with 1x sampling and no PsParamGen. Shaders with
+dynamic register addressing are left to play. Guesses that no draw asks for only cost file space; draws that ask
+for something else are translated as before and stored.
+
+## 4. The file
+
+Host byte order. `Header` (48 bytes: magic `NXRCACHE`, version 3, `sizeof(PipelineState)`, translation
+environment, entry count, index offset), then 16-byte aligned blobs, then the index (32-byte entries sorted by
+kind, type, key, sub: `{kind, type, size, key, sub, offset}`), then the journal: `{entry, bytes}` records appended
+since the file was last written whole (a record cut short ends the file; the next whole write drops it).
+
+| Kind | Key / sub | Bytes |
+|---|---|---|
+| `S` shader | microcode hash / – (type: `xenos::ShaderType`) | big-endian microcode |
+| `T` translation | hash / modification | the translator's SPIR-V (before `PrepareForDiligent`) |
+| `P` pipeline | XXH3 of the record | `PipelineRecord` (shader hashes and modifications, `PipelineState` with null shaders) |
+| `V` driver cache | – | DiligentCore's `IPipelineStateCache` data (Vulkan's pipeline cache) |
+| `G` game scanned | the game's fingerprint | – |
+
+Invalidation: another header version or `PipelineState` size starts the file again; another **translation
+environment** (`ShaderCache::environment()`: the render scale the translator reverts, the SPIR-V version, a tag
+to bump when NativeXRenderer changes what it gives the translator) drops `T` and `G` records only, so the shaders
+are translated again (and the game's shaders guessed again) but the pipelines are kept. The driver ignores a cache
+from another GPU or driver by itself. Bump `kVersion` in `storage.cpp` when a record's meaning changes
+(`PipelineState`'s layout or enums, the modification bits, host formats).
+
+Sizes (Mad Riders, first launch): 885 shipped shaders, a few thousand pixel translations; expect tens of MB with
+the driver's cache.
+
+## 5. Changing it
+
+- A new kind of record: add a `Kind`, write it with `AddLocked` (journaled) or like `SetDriverCache` (only with the
+  whole file), read it in `Open`'s final loop.
+- Better guesses: `QueueGameTranslations` (draw.cpp). The draw-time modification code is in `DrawInternal`; keep
+  the two in step. Bump the environment tag in `ShaderCache::environment()` so old guesses are redone.
+- Vertex shaders ahead of play would need the game's vertex declarations: the D3D patch is deterministic per
+  (shader, declaration), so a game that can list its declarations could patch the shipped microcode itself and
+  hand over the variants as `GameShader`s.
